        <h1>A Brief History of Operating Systems</h1>

        <figure class="page-graphic">
            <img src="/img/os-timeline.svg" alt="Timeline showing operating-system development from batch computing to connected devices">
            <figcaption>Operating systems evolved as computers became more interactive and widely available.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p>Operating systems have changed as computers have evolved. Their development moved from batch processing to interactive machines, personal devices, and connected services.</p>

        <h2>1950s: Batch processing</h2>
        <p>Early computers processed groups of jobs in sequence. Operators prepared work for the machine, and direct interactive use was limited.</p>

        <h2>1960s–1970s: Sharing computing time</h2>
        <p>Time-sharing systems let multiple people interact with a computer through terminals. Unix also introduced ideas that influenced many later operating systems.</p>

        <h2>1980s–1990s: Personal computers</h2>
        <p>Personal computers became more common. Graphical interfaces made it easier to work with files, windows, and applications visually.</p>

        <h2>2000s to today: Connected devices</h2>
        <p>Smartphones, cloud services, and embedded computers brought operating systems into everyday devices and made network access a central part of computing.</p>

        <h2>A continuing role</h2>
        <p>As hardware changed, the operating system kept its central role: coordinating computer resources and giving people and programs a useful way to work with the machine.</p>

        <section class="book-source">
            <h2>Book source</h2>
